use ../../lib/UiComponent

export ui-component Link:
    description:
        A link takes the user somewhere else, either another place in the app
        or outside it. It is usually text, and may sit in the middle of a
        sentence.

    destination:
        A link always has a destination, and the user can find out where it
        goes before following it, such as by hovering over it. How that is
        shown is up to the chosen library and platform.

    content:
        Usually text, sometimes with an icon. Its name says where it goes,
        well enough to make sense read out on its own. "Read the pricing
        guide" works; "click here" doesn't.

    activation:
        The user follows a link the usual way for the platform: clicking,
        tapping, or pressing Enter. Which keys do this is up to the chosen
        library.

    newContext:
        The component using the link decides whether it opens in the current
        view or a new one, such as a new tab or window. Opening somewhere new
        is made clear to the user. The user can still choose for themselves
        wherever the platform lets them, such as by middle-clicking.

    current:
        A link to the page or place the user is already on, such as in a
        NavigationMenu or Pagination, can be marked as current. It is shown as
        current and assistive tools are told so. Whether it can still be
        followed is up to the component using it.

    visited:
        Whether a link shows that it has already been followed is up to the
        chosen library.

    nonGoals:
        - Links don't perform actions. Something that does a thing rather than
            going somewhere is a Button, even if it looks like a link
        - No disabled state. A link with nowhere to go should be shown as plain
            text instead
